  "answer_markdown": "**gedoor/legado should currently be considered only for source-availability qualification.** The catalog snapshot dated 2026-09-01 describes an Android reader with configurable book sources, matching the project goal. However, the supplied host observation dated 2026-10-11 reports that the checked default branch contains README.md and an announcement image, and that the README announces removal of the project content. This weakens the basis for choosing it: available application code is currently unconfirmed; other branches and releases have not been verified. Photo managers and media servers do not replace the required reader. These are supplied observations, not my network request or a catalog refresh. [Source: gedoor/legado](https://github.com/gedoor/legado).\n\nThe proposed first step is an isolated check for usable source, its provenance and its license. Only after that check succeeds can an Android pilot with one configurable, permitted book source be proposed, without connecting it to the main application's data. Legado's possible role is to provide a reader and source-configuration implementation; its actual availability remains unconfirmed. The team owns compatibility, content rights, integration, user-data storage and maintenance. For the same pilot scope, compare the existing source mechanism, if one exists, and a minimal custom adapter: there is no evidence that reuse requires less effort.\n\nThe sole planning scenario is **6–10 engineer-hours for first validation**—a hypothetical capped evaluation slice, not a measurement or delivery commitment. Prerequisites: first check whether usable source is available; runnable reader code is not assumed. One engineer familiar with Android application development is allocated, at 8 hours per engineer-day; confidence is low. Complete adoption effort and the schedule in working days are unknown. Source availability and licensing, build reproducibility, source format and application compatibility would change the decision.\n\nThe first-validation acceptance check is to establish usable source and a license permitting the intended use; otherwise, stop at source-availability qualification. After that, the acceptance check for a separately authorized pilot is a reproducible build and a demonstration of configuring one source to retrieve and display test book content. Safe rollback means disabling the isolated pilot while preserving the main application, library and user data; the existing reading path is unknown, so its restoration cannot be promised. The next bounded coding task the user could separately authorize after source and license verification is to create an isolated Android pilot for one source and test that scenario. Nothing has been executed; an adoption recommendation remains on hold.",
